 ## Evidence
 
-- Implementation pending.
+- `file-io::paged_service::PagedSession` is the shared owner for the paged actor, path/fingerprint, opaque savepoint, encoding/conflict/cleanup state, recovery configuration/status/discard/cleanup hold, source generations, tail session, spill state, and operation generation. `PagedEditorSurface` no longer stores duplicate path, fingerprint, saved-state, save-as, recovery, tail, retired-generation, or spill owners.
+- `PagedReadHandle` moved to file-io. It is renderer-free, retains the session and cleanup hold transitively, resolves current and historical source pages, and exposes typed `Busy`/`Changed`/`SourceUnavailable` outcomes before the display adapter renders text.
+- Save and Save Copy run through `execute_save`, which validates document identity, revision, content state, source generation, and operation generation before writing and again before publishing. The typed terminal receipt retains `Cancelled`, `Encoding`, `Conflict`, `SourceUnavailable`, and cleanup state; displaced/proposed-byte artifacts remain owned by the unchanged T04 save primitive.
+- Recovery resume installs a fresh status owner and increments the operation generation. Every peer and captured read handle owns the same session, so the T05 `CleanupHold` remains live until the final view/read owner drops; durable discard and physical cleanup remain separate in `recovery_retirement`.
+- Cross-document transfer and linked undo still acquire all document-only session guards before publication and use the unchanged grouped recovery commit/lease primitives.
+- Added a renderer-free file-io lifecycle contract that Save Copy preserves UTF-8 BOM/CRLF bytes exactly, does not change the source identity, and rejects stale receipt application. Extended the controlled peer-view test so a stale lifecycle save command cannot write or change presentation state.
+- Lightweight local evidence: direct `rustfmt --edition 2024` on changed Rust files and `git diff --check` pass. Cargo and native checks remain central by policy.
 
